    <div class="content-panel">
      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">Step 1: Optimize your profile first — everything else builds on this</h2>
      <p>The single biggest mistake in LinkedIn job searching is outreaching and applying before optimizing the profile. When a recruiter gets your message, receives your application, or sees your comment on a post — the first thing they do is click your profile. If that profile does not immediately communicate your level, your specialization, and your fit, the click converts to nothing.</p>
      <p>Profile optimization is not cosmetic. It is the foundation that determines whether every other action you take — networking, applying, commenting, messaging — generates any return.</p>
      <p>The three sections that matter most: (1) Your headline — the most keyword-weighted field, visible in every search result. (2) Your About section — the hook that turns a click into genuine interest. (3) Your most recent experience description — the first thing a recruiter reads after your headline and photo.</p>
      <p>Do not start any other activity until all three are optimized for your target role.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-panel">
      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">Step 2: Open to Work — recruiter-only mode, specific titles</h2>
      <p>Open to Work in recruiter-only mode signals your availability to LinkedIn Recruiter (the paid tool recruiters use) without displaying the green banner to the public — including your current employer. This is the standard setting for active job seekers who are currently employed.</p>
      <p>The critical mistake: listing "open to opportunities" in the job title field when setting up Open to Work. This is not a searchable job title — recruiters search for specific role titles. Instead, list 2–3 specific titles that match what you are targeting:</p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Right: "Senior Software Engineer," "Staff Software Engineer," "Platform Engineer"</li>
        <li>Wrong: "Open to new opportunities," "Seeking next challenge"</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Set your preferred locations specifically (remote, specific metro areas). Set your start date to "Immediately" or a specific date if you have a notice period — vague answers slow recruiter outreach.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-tight">
  <div class="container">
    <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow section-title-center">The daily LinkedIn job search framework</h2>
    <p class="section-subtitle" style="text-align:center; margin-bottom:32px;">30–45 minutes of structured daily activity outperforms 3-hour weekend sessions. Here is the daily breakdown that drives results.</p>
    <div class="two-col-grid">
      <div class="content-panel">
        <h3>Morning (15–20 min)</h3>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li><strong>Respond to recruiter messages within 24 hours.</strong> Response speed signals active engagement. A delayed response — especially to an inbound message — frequently loses the opportunity to a faster candidate.</li>
          <li><strong>Check new job postings.</strong> Sort by "Most recent" in LinkedIn Jobs. Applications within 24–48 hours of posting receive 2–3x higher response rates than applications submitted after a week.</li>
          <li><strong>Review connection request acceptances.</strong> When someone accepts, send a brief follow-up within 24 hours while you are top of mind.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3 style="margin-top:24px;">Midday (5–10 min)</h3>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li><strong>Comment on 2–3 posts</strong> from people at target companies or in your target roles. Substantive comments (a genuine perspective, a data point, a question) build visibility. "Great post!" does not.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="content-panel">
        <h3>Outreach block (15–20 min, 3x per week)</h3>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li><strong>Send 3–5 targeted connection requests</strong> per outreach session — each with a specific, personalized note. Quality over volume.</li>
          <li><strong>Follow up on pending conversations.</strong> If you sent a connection request or message 5+ days ago with no response, a brief one-line follow-up is appropriate — once.</li>
          <li><strong>Identify 2–3 new targets</strong> for the next outreach session. Research their profiles, find a specific hook, and draft the message before closing LinkedIn.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3 style="margin-top:24px;">Weekly (30 min)</h3>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li><strong>Check Search Appearances</strong> in your LinkedIn dashboard — how many times did your profile appear in recruiter searches? Is it growing?</li>
          <li><strong>Update your application tracker</strong> — what applications need follow-up? What conversations are stalled?</li>
          <li><strong>Review your messaging hit rate</strong> — if fewer than 15% of your connection requests are accepted, the message or targeting needs adjustment.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-tight">
  <div class="container">
    <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow section-title-center">Outreach strategy — what gets responses</h2>
    <div class="two-col-grid" style="margin-top:28px;">
      <div class="content-panel">
        <h3>The connection request (300-character note)</h3>
        <p>The LinkedIn connection request note has a 300-character limit. Every character counts. The framework: who you are in one line + a specific reference to them + an easy, low-commitment close.</p>
        <p>Strong examples:</p>
        <ul style="list-style:none; padding:0;">
          <li style="padding:12px 14px; margin-bottom:8px; background:rgba(87,207,196,0.06); border-radius:8px; border-left:3px solid rgba(87,207,196,0.3); font-size:0.88rem;">Hi [Name], I'm a staff engineer moving into distributed systems at scale — came across your post on [topic] and found it genuinely useful. Happy to connect.</li>
          <li style="padding:12px 14px; margin-bottom:8px; background:rgba(87,207,196,0.06); border-radius:8px; border-left:3px solid rgba(87,207,196,0.3); font-size:0.88rem;">Hi [Name], senior PM at [Company] here — we have a shared connection in [Name]. I've been following [Company]'s expansion into [area] and would love to be in your network.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 style="margin-top:24px;">Targeting — who to reach out to</h3>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li><strong>Hiring managers</strong> at target companies — search "[Role] Manager" or "Director of [function]" at target companies. A direct message about their team often outperforms an ATS application.</li>
          <li><strong>Recruiters at target companies</strong> — search "Recruiter" or "Talent Acquisition" at companies you are targeting. Internal recruiters are higher-value than agency recruiters for specific roles.</li>
          <li><strong>Alumni from your school or previous employers</strong> at target companies — shared context increases acceptance and response rates significantly.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="content-panel">
        <h3>LinkedIn Jobs — how to search effectively</h3>
        <p>Most professionals use LinkedIn Jobs inefficiently. The settings that matter:</p>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li><strong>Sort by "Most recent"</strong> — not "Most relevant." Most relevant buries new postings. You want to be in the first 50 applicants, not the 500th.</li>
          <li><strong>Filter by "Easy Apply" off</strong> — Easy Apply roles often have 500+ applicants. Direct applications through the company website have meaningfully lower competition.</li>
          <li><strong>Set job alerts for exact title searches</strong> — "Staff Software Engineer," not just "Software Engineer." Specific alerts surface the right roles without noise.</li>
          <li><strong>Use LinkedIn's "Follow" feature for target companies</strong> — you will see job postings in your feed before they are heavily circulated, giving you the early-applicant advantage.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3 style="margin-top:24px;">Apply on the company website</h3>
        <p>When you find a role on LinkedIn, apply on the company careers page — not through LinkedIn's "Easy Apply." Reasons: (1) Company-site applications signal higher intent. (2) They route directly into the ATS with your formatting intact. (3) Easy Apply submissions are frequently deprioritized in favor of direct applicants by hiring teams that are overwhelmed with volume.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-tight">
  <div class="container">
    <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow section-title-center">The mistakes that cost you inbound</h2>
    <div class="two-col-grid" style="margin-top:28px;">
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Applying without an optimized profile.</strong> Every application you submit sends a recruiter to your LinkedIn profile. If that profile does not reinforce your application, the application loses credibility. Optimize first.</li>
        <li><strong>Generic connection requests.</strong> "I'd like to add you to my professional network" has a less than 20% acceptance rate. Personalized requests referencing something specific about the recipient average 60–70%.</li>
        <li><strong>Asking for a job in the first message.</strong> The first message should build rapport or ask a small, easy question. Leading with a job ask — especially before connecting — kills the conversation before it starts.</li>
        <li><strong>Applying weeks after posting.</strong> Response rates for applications submitted within 24–48 hours of a posting are 2–3x higher than later applications. Set up alerts and apply early.</li>
      </ul>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Not responding to recruiter InMail quickly.</strong> Recruiter pipelines move fast. A response after 48 hours often means the recruiter has moved to the next candidate. Check LinkedIn daily during an active search.</li>
        <li><strong>No Open to Work signal.</strong> If you are not signaling availability, you are invisible to recruiters using the Open to Work filter — a filter most use to narrow their candidate search.</li>
        <li><strong>Treating LinkedIn like a job board instead of a network.</strong> The most valuable LinkedIn channel is not job postings — it is the relationships that get you warm introductions, referrals, and conversations before a role is ever posted.</li>
        <li><strong>Posting without substance.</strong> Vague "thought leadership" posts have minimal impact. If you post, make it specific: a lesson learned, a data point, a genuine perspective on something in your domain. Specificity builds credibility.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

